msgid "Trace an anomaly on a slice"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Read an anomaly on the slices of a geophysical stack in the Space, and "
"record it as a unit with the slices it was read on.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An anomaly on a slice is a reading: *here*, at this depth, the signal "
"changes. The **Geofisica** package records it as on an image (see :doc"
":`trace-regions-on-images`): a stratigraphic unit, **USD**, whose "
"properties come from an **extractor** of the slice, with the place of the"
" anomaly on it. When the anomaly is read on several slices, a "
"**combiner** gathers one extractor for each slice. What the anomaly *is* "
"stays your judgement: the unit says the shape read and the slices, "
"nothing more."
msgstr ""

msgid "Before you start"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Bring the stack into the study and show it in the **Space** (see :doc"
":`/how-to/bring-in/bring-in-a-geophysical-stack`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Go with the depth cursor to the slice where the anomaly shows."
msgstr ""

msgid "Trace"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Over the scene, beside the depth cursor, a bar offers the three tools of "
"the package: **◉** **Point anomaly on a slice**, **〰** **Linear anomaly "
"on a slice** and **▱** **Areal anomaly on a slice**. The bar is there "
"while a stack of the package is shown."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Space seen from above on a slice of the Basilica Iulia: the bar Areal"
" anomaly on a slice: 3 points with its tick and cross, and below it the "
"depth cursor at D.03 · 3.00–3.50 m below ground (3/6)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Press the tool of the shape you read."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Click on the slice: each click is a point of the anomaly, drawn at once "
"on the plane in yellow, and the bar counts them, for example **Linear "
"anomaly on a slice: 2 points**. A click elsewhere than on a slice of the "
"stack is answered **Click on a slice of the stack**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A point is written at the first click. Close a line or an area with a "
"double click, with **✓** or with :kbd:`Enter`."
msgstr ""

msgid "To stop without writing, press **✕** or :kbd:`Esc`: the yellow points go."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A slice of the Basilica Iulia seen from above: a line of three yellow "
"points being traced, the bar Linear anomaly on a slice: 3 points with its"
" tick and cross, and a cyan line of an anomaly already written."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Once written, the anomaly stays drawn on the plane in cyan, on each slice"
" it was read on: a dot, a line, or the closed outline of an area, with "
"its vertices."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The same slice after the tick: the new line is drawn in cyan beside the "
"earlier anomaly, and the bar Linear anomaly on a slice: 0 points waits "
"for the next trace."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The trace is written as one step: ``Edit ▸ Undo`` takes it all back. The "
"same trace written twice adds nothing. A tool stays armed only on its "
"document: when the Space shows another study, it is put down."
msgstr ""

msgid "Read the anomaly on several slices"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An anomaly often runs through several depths. Two ways gather them in one"
" reading:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"click on planes of different slices of the same stack, with **Down to** "
"in **Visibility ▾** so that they are shown together;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"with **Down to**, the slices shown down to the cursor's are read too, at "
"the same place."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The unit then gets a **combiner**, with one extractor for each slice. "
"Only the horizontal slices of the same stack are taken; a trace on a "
"vertical section stays on that section."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A new unit **USD**, with the kind of the anomaly read: point, linear or "
"areal, after the tool. The vocabulary of the package also has "
"**diffraction hyperbola**, **high-resistivity zone** and **low-"
"resistivity zone**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Its properties: the kind, the method and the depth band, each with the "
"slice as its provenance."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An **extractor** of the slice, with the anomaly's place in metres of the "
"stack, on the slice's file; or a **combiner** of the extractors of "
"several slices."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Select the unit in the **Outliner** and look at the **Inspector**: the "
"section **Geophysical reading** says on how many slices it was read, "
"through which extractor or combiner, the depth bands and the slices."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The Geophysical reading section of the Inspector for an anomaly: Read on "
"2 geophysical slices, Through 1 combiner, Bands 1.00–3.00 m below ground,"
" and the two slices D.01 and D.02."
msgstr ""

msgid "See also"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`trace-regions-on-images`, the same reading on a photograph or a "
"drawing."
msgstr ""

msgid ":doc:`/reference/geofisica`, the tools and the Inspector section."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
":doc:`/explanation/slice-as-document`, the unit, the extractor and the "
"slice."
msgstr ""

